WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.398
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to DataVis DevTalk."

00:00:02.398 --> 00:00:03.880
I'm your host, Minhaz Kazi.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.210
Our today's guest
is Ben Collins.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.960
Ben is a freelance data
analyst, developer, visualizer

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.120
and founder of the
Collins School of Data.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.450
He's also a Google
Developer expert.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:15.200
Let's get started.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.108
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.440
Ben, welcome to the show.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:22.920
BEN COLLINS: Thank you, Minhaz.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:23.960
Thank you for having me.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.550
MINHAZ KAZI: Tell us a
little bit about yourself.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:27.980
What got you interested in
spreadsheets and scripting

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:28.920
in the first place?

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:29.670
BEN COLLINS: Sure.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.170
So after college, I went
into the finance world.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.360
And obviously, my bread and
butter then is spreadsheets.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.753
So I'm spending all day
with data in spreadsheets

00:00:40.753 --> 00:00:42.170
with formulas, and
gradually, just

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.545
became more and more comfortable
with the formulas world.

00:00:44.545 --> 00:00:46.253
And it's a little bit
like a rabbit hole.

00:00:46.253 --> 00:00:48.500
The deeper you get into
it, the more you discover.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.990
And so the formulas
became sort of,

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.900
let's find out how I can make
these formulas more efficient

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.230
and make my work better.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.720
And then that led into the
world of scripting, and saying,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.220
OK, this is really interesting.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.450
How can I automate these
processes I'm doing

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.030
and extend power of the
spreadsheets in my work?

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.410
So that was my first
foray with spreadsheets,

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.360
and that was sort of, started
15 years ago, that journey,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.590
I guess.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:20.720
And then sort of
in 2010-ish, I came

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.000
across G Suite and the
Google Sheets world,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.265
and made that step across into
G Suite and Google Sheets,

00:01:27.265 --> 00:01:29.140
and found Apps Script
pretty soon after that.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.210
So I could continue that journey
of learning and getting more

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.373
from my spreadsheets.

00:01:33.373 --> 00:01:35.290
MINHAZ KAZI: So I have
to ask you this though,

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.212
VLOOKUP or INDEX MATCH?

00:01:37.212 --> 00:01:38.420
BEN COLLINS: A good question.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.250
And I've got feet in
both camps, I'd say,

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.730
because I think that the VLOOKUP
is a great one when you just

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.190
need to do something
quick and easy.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.800
It works, it's great, as long
as you know how to use it.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.677
And then the INDEX MATCH gives
you a lot more flexibility.

00:01:52.677 --> 00:01:55.010
It seems to be the question
that divides the spreadsheet

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:56.430
world in two.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.640
And I know that
in my old office,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.780
we had camps in both,
advocates for both,

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.690
and there were pretty
fierce advocates for each.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:09.270
But I think that I
would say learn both.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.660
Go and do the VLOOKUP,
and then really, it's

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.180
not too difficult to jump
across and learn INDEX MATCH.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.450
And that opens up tons
more opportunities

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.690
with what you can do with
the INDEX and the MATCH,

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.140
both separately and together.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.840
MINHAZ KAZI: So let's
say I'm an analyst

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.840
and I don't know anything
about Apps Script.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.430
Why is it important for me?

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.710
And how do I get started?

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:30.460
BEN COLLINS: Sure.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:35.510
So let's say every week
you create a report,

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.170
and you apply the
same formatting,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:41.510
you create the same chart, you
do a little bit of preparation

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.150
with your data, and
each week it's the same.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:47.750
And you're sort of spending 15
minutes, half an hour, an hour,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.010
doing this pretty
repetitive process.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.630
Then that's a great
contender for automation.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.930
And that's what scripts
allow you to do.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.220
So with just a
few lines of code,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.650
you can transform some data.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.200
And then a few more lines of
code, you can create a chart.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.320
And that's the power
of Apps Script.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.940
You write that once, and then
each week, you can press a row

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.735
and it will do
those steps for you.

00:03:09.735 --> 00:03:11.360
So a great way to
get started actually,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.490
is to use the macro recorder.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:17.850
And what that is, is a
built-in feature in the UI,

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.700
so in the actual
spreadsheet itself

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.680
where you can press
the big red button,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.250
do your thing, which is
sort of add some formatting,

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.640
create the chart, process your
data, and then press stop.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.030
And the macro
recorder turns that

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:35.180
into code in the
script editor, which

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.950
you can then just
go and run when

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.250
you want to repeat the action.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.792
So that's a great
way to get started.

00:03:39.792 --> 00:03:43.100
And then from there, you can
open up the script editor

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.700
and see exactly the code that
the macro recorder has created,

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.027
and you'll start to see, even
if you don't understand it

00:03:50.027 --> 00:03:51.860
at first, you'll start
to recognize sections

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:54.210
where they perhaps, you'll
see some cell references.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.210
And then you'll start to
understand a little bit

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.160
about how that
code is interacting

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.390
with the spreadsheets.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.560
Another way is custom functions.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.080
So custom functions
are a way for you

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.540
to write a little
bit of Apps Script

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.820
to do something
that's completely

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:15.061
unique to your workflow.

00:04:15.061 --> 00:04:16.519
So you might create
a function that

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:20.700
uses Google Maps to work out
the distance between two points,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.670
and you can just use
that function then just

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:25.370
in your Google Sheets, without
needing to keep rewriting code

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:26.955
or going to
external, to the maps

00:04:26.955 --> 00:04:29.330
and doing that separately and
then copying and pasting it

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:30.247
back in sort of thing.

00:04:30.247 --> 00:04:31.940
So it's a lot of time saving.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.460
MINHAZ KAZI: Switching
gears, how did you

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.020
get started with Data Studio?

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.620
BEN COLLINS: So I
remember back in 2016,

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.780
I was doing a lot of
work with Google Sheets.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.180
And then up pops onto
the radar this new tool

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.180
coming from Google that's
a business intelligence

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.640
tool for the self serve.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.350
In other words,
something that you're

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:57.140
going to be able to use
yourself to create these

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.102
visualizations and reports.

00:04:59.102 --> 00:05:00.560
And so I was just
super interested.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.768
Because I'd worked in this
space for a long time now,

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:05.180
and so I'm always sort of
interested in new tools,

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.960
and especially ones
that will extend

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.510
the capabilities of what
I'm doing with the tools

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:12.480
I have right now.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.050
And so I was super
interested, jumped on it

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.000
and got involved
with a beta program

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.440
and collaborated with
you on some feedback,

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.480
and wrote some blog posts,
recorded some videos,

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.587
and that's sort of how it all
sort of got started for me.

00:05:26.587 --> 00:05:28.670
MINHAZ KAZI: One question
I often hear from folks,

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:31.920
is that you can always
build graphs and charts

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.950
in spreadsheets
and can share that.

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:37.760
What is the benefit of having
a dedicated business analytics

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.558
tool or reporting
tool like Data Studio?

00:05:39.558 --> 00:05:41.100
BEN COLLINS: That's
a great question.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.560
And I think one of the principle
benefits is, first of all,

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.490
it's very simple and easy to
use to create very professional

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:50.870
looking reports.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:53.030
So with a spreadsheet,
it's a little bit more work

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:57.013
to make it look like
a report, a dashboard,

00:05:57.013 --> 00:05:59.430
rather than a spreadsheet that
just has some charts in it.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:04.490
So maybe that's important, so
that your stakeholders look

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:06.260
at the report and
take it a little more

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.885
seriously than just perhaps it's
a thrown together spreadsheet.

00:06:08.885 --> 00:06:12.900
So it makes your professional
reports very easy to create.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:17.420
Another huge benefit is,
it separates the data

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.630
from the visualization.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:21.650
By having a dedicated
report that people

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:24.500
interact with, with the
data in a separate layer

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:25.730
that they can't touch.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:28.498
It just make sure that that
data is nice and secure.

00:06:28.498 --> 00:06:30.290
MINHAZ KAZI: You have
the unique capability

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:33.650
of looking at this from both
an analyst's point of view

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:35.960
and from a developer
point of view.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.210
Thinking from that
angle, how do you

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:42.470
think Data Studio can be
beneficial to small and medium

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:43.015
businesses?

00:06:43.015 --> 00:06:45.140
BEN COLLINS: What it does
is, it democratizes data.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.120
It opens up the data
to so many more people.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:54.238
And so both building with Data
Studio or being given a report

00:06:54.238 --> 00:06:56.030
and interacting with
it and using a report,

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:59.270
are both very, very
intuitive and easy.

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:03.170
They have that same interface
as the G Suite tools,

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.450
so it sort of feels
familiar already.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:07.130
And then building
the actual report

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.807
is really quick and really easy.

00:07:09.807 --> 00:07:12.140
And so it really lowers that
barrier to entry for people

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.600
to go and explore
their own data.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.060
And then also just
using those reports.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:20.000
If a report is shared with you,
it gives you a lot of options

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.760
to interact with the
data, to drill down

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.688
into the data in
a very visual way.

00:07:25.688 --> 00:07:27.230
MINHAZ KAZI: I love
the demo that you

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:30.580
built with the International
Space Station position,

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:34.690
so you could see the position
of ISS at any point of time,

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:36.467
like where it was.

00:07:36.467 --> 00:07:37.800
Tell us a little bit about that.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:38.810
How did you build that?

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:39.560
BEN COLLINS: Sure.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:42.380
So it was right around the
time actually that Google

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.570
was introducing the
Community Connectors, which

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:47.960
are the connectors that you
can connect to third party

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:50.570
services with Data
Studio directly and pull

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.860
in that data from
another web service

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.770
into Data Studio, which
is like super cool.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:55.970
I was excited about this.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:59.180
And I was looking
around for some examples

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:02.150
to just try this
out, because there

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:05.010
was a couple of examples
on Google's websites,

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:06.663
which I tried.

00:08:06.663 --> 00:08:08.080
And then I thought,
you know what?

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.190
I wonder if there's a
way to plot the position.

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:14.900
I'm always interested
in space stuff.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.232
I follow the rocket launches
and that kind of stuff,

00:08:17.232 --> 00:08:18.440
so I'm a bit of a space geek.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:20.270
I searched around,
found an API that

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.400
publishes the position
in latitude and longitude

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:25.080
of the International
Space Station and thought,

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.000
let's see if we
can make this work.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.600
And it was a great
first connector

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:32.554
to build, because the
authentication was,

00:08:32.554 --> 00:08:35.179
there was no authentication, so
it was a nice simple connector.

00:08:35.179 --> 00:08:38.690
Pull in that lat and long,
and plot it on the world map

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:41.630
in a nice dashboard that had
some touches, like a picture

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:44.630
and sort of a current date
and things just to make

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:46.320
it look a little more polished.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.695
MINHAZ KAZI: You have built
a number of these connectors,

00:08:48.695 --> 00:08:50.787
both as like open source,
and for your clients.

00:08:50.787 --> 00:08:52.370
What does your
workflow look like when

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.800
you're trying to build a
connector for a new data set?

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:55.550
Where do we start?

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:56.755
What is your workflow?

00:08:56.755 --> 00:08:58.130
BEN COLLINS: So
I would, I always

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:00.770
start by spending some
time with that service.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:01.722
And it depends.

00:09:01.722 --> 00:09:03.680
If it's a service that
I use myself, obviously,

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:04.680
I'd be familiar with it.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:07.490
But if it's a client
that I'm working for

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.020
and it's their
service or a service

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:10.610
they use, then what
I like to do is,

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:13.460
make sure I go and
get a demo account,

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:15.230
spend some time
with the tool, so I

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:18.080
understand what the context is.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.860
And I can start to
understand why would somebody

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.860
want that data in
Data Studio and what

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:25.110
are their plans to do with it.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:26.610
I think it's very
important that you

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.090
have that context before
you just jump in and rush

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:30.670
to build the
connector, so that you

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.470
know when you bring that data
through that pipeline, where

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.418
it's going to end up and
what it might look like.

00:09:36.418 --> 00:09:38.960
So I start with the tool, the
service, spend some time there.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:42.938
Next thing is to go and find
any documentation about the API.

00:09:42.938 --> 00:09:44.480
Spend time reading
that, see if there

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.930
are any tutorials or guides
to just have a look at

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:48.710
and understand.

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:52.730
And then what I do is, I open
up a Google Sheet and the script

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:55.670
editor and build a
connection to the API

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:57.860
from my Google Sheet
first, and then

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:00.080
start putting the data
into Google Sheets.

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:02.390
And the reason I do
that, is because I

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:06.120
don't need to worry then about
the Data Studio architecture

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:06.620
yet.

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:10.070
I can focus purely on the
connection to the API,

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:13.670
start getting some data back,
maybe check the authentication

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:15.060
and how that works.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:18.900
And then start parsing
that data and making sure

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:23.380
that I've got the API piece all
built and sort of rock solid.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:25.685
Then I can open up Data
Studio and, or rather

00:10:25.685 --> 00:10:28.060
the Apps Script for Data
Studio, put in the boiler plate,

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.780
build the plumbing, and then
plug in the API piece finally.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:33.693
And that's my
approach that rather

00:10:33.693 --> 00:10:35.110
than trying to
sort of immediately

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:38.113
jump to Data Studio file
and build it in one go.

00:10:38.113 --> 00:10:40.030
MINHAZ KAZI: So hopefully
some of our audience

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:42.060
would benefit from that.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:43.840
Talking about the
audience, can you

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.230
share your favorite Data
Studio tip with them?

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:46.980
BEN COLLINS: Sure.

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:48.688
So I was thinking
about this a little bit

00:10:48.688 --> 00:10:51.910
before I came in today,
and I was racking my brain

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:54.050
for something super
specific, maybe

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:55.860
a neat little unique
feature or something.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:57.610
And then I sort of
realized that actually,

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:01.810
my top tip really is to
spend some time learning

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:05.560
about the best practices
for data visualization.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:10.170
So knowing the best chart type
to use for the data you have.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.645
How to make sure that
your dashboard is

00:11:12.645 --> 00:11:14.957
as impactful as possible.

00:11:14.957 --> 00:11:16.540
Because that will
have a bigger impact

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:20.830
than sort of focusing too much
on the tiny granular details,

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:24.520
if you really can make sure
that your data pops off the page

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:26.980
and gets that message across
as efficiently and effectively

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:29.200
as possible, then you'll
get maximum impact

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.262
from your dashboards.

